===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|id=able to|inh|enm>-able>able to}}
{{inh+|en|enm|-able}}, borrowed from {{der|en|fro|-able}}, from {{der|en|la|-ābilis}}, from {{m|la|-a-}} or {{m|la|-i-}} + {{m|la|-bilis||capable or worthy of being acted upon}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|-}} i-stem form {{m|ine-pro|*-dʰli-}} of {{m|ine-pro|*-dʰlom||instrumental suffix}}. Not closely related etymologically, though currently related semantically, to ''[[able]]''. Displaced native {{ncog|ang|-endlīc}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ə.bl̩/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl--able.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Suffix===
{{en-suffix|cat2=adjective-forming suffixes}}

# {{non-gloss|An [[adjectival]] [[suffix]]; forms adjectives meaning:}}
## [[able|Able]] to be [[done]]; fit to be done.
##: mov'''able''': able to be moved
##: amend'''able''': able to be amended
##: break'''able''': liable to broken
##: blam'''able''': fit to be blamed
##: sal'''able''': fit to be sold
## Relevant to or suitable to, in accordance with.
##: fashion'''able''': relevant to fashion
##: season'''able''': suitable to season
## Giving, or inclined to.
##: pleasur'''able''': giving pleasure
##: peace'''able''': inclined to peace
## Subject to.
##: report'''able''': subject to be reported
##: tax'''able''': subject to be taxed
## Due to be.
##: pay'''able''': due to be paid

====Usage notes====
* Originally appeared only on French and Latin words, like {{m|en|separable}}. Over time ''-able'' was added to stems of English verbs ending in ''-ate'', such as {{m|en|educable}}. Finally, due to probable confusion with the word {{m|en|able}}, it was used to form adjectives from all sorts of verbs, nouns, and even verb phrases, such as {{m|en|kickable}}, {{m|en|get-at-able}}, and {{m|en|hittable}}.
* A terminal silent ''-e'' is often dropped when adding ''-able'', but for roots ending with a soft ''-ce'' or ''-ge'', such as {{m|en|replaceable}} and {{m|en|changeable}}, the ''-e'' is kept so that these are not misinterpreted as hard ‘c’ or ‘g’ sounds. Similar spelling patterns apply to some other suffixes beginning with a vowel, such as ''-ous'' in {{m|en|famous}} vs. {{m|en|courageous}}.
* The final consonant of a root is doubled in the same contexts as when adding the suffix {{m|en|-ed}}. In general, this means doubling occurs when the preceding vowel is short and stressed (as in {{m|en|winnable}}) but not when it is long (as in {{m|en|obtainable}}) or unstressed (as in {{m|en|openable}}). In British English, a final L is typically doubled after a short vowel regardless of whether the vowel is stressed or unstressed (as in {{m|en|compellable}}, {{m|en|modellable}}). In American English, final L typically follows the same rules as other consonants (as in {{m|en|compellable}}, {{m|en|modelable}}). These are the general trends, but there is some variation within British and American English between these two methods of doubling final L.
* The form {{m|en|-ible}} usually has the same senses and pronunciation, though sometimes equivalent terms have diverged in meaning: compare {{m|en|suggestable||capable of being suggested}} with {{m|en|suggestible||susceptible to influence by suggestion}}. The choice between the two is somewhat idiosyncratic, but in general, {{m|en||-ible}} is used in forms derived from Latin verbs of the second, third, and fourth conjugations, and in a few words whose roots end in a soft ''c'' or ''g'', while {{m|en||-able}} is used in all other words, particularly those formed from Latin verbs of the first conjugation and those that come from French or from Anglo-Saxon (Old English). Fowler's ''English Usage'' recommends using {{m|en||-ible}} for simplicity's sake in ''any'' word whose root ends in a soft ''c'' or ''g'' to avoid ''-eable'' (e.g., {{m|en||*changible}} rather than {{m|en|changeable}}), but this recommendation has generally not been followed.
* A number of adjectives in {{m|en||-able}} come from verbs that do not have direct objects, but that rather are construed with prepositions. In these cases, the preposition does not appear with the adjective in {{m|en||-able}}; hence, {{m|en|reliable||fit to being [[rely|relied]] on}}, {{m|en|laughable||suited for [[laugh]]ing at}}, {{m|en|remarkable||fit to be [[remark]]ed upon}}, and so on.
* Traditionally, verbs ending in {{m|en|-ate}} drop this suffix before adding {{m|en||-able}}; hence, {{m|en|communicable||able to be [[communicate]]d}}, {{m|en|eradicable||possible to [[eradicate]]}}, {{m|en|implacable||unable to be [[placated]]}}, {{m|en|inimitable||unable to be [[imitate]]d}}, and so on, but {{m|en|relatable}}, because ''relate'' is ''re-'' + ''-late'', not ''rel-'' + ''-ate''. Logically one should therefore say ''[[rotable]]'' to mean "able to be rotated", but {{m|en|rotatable}} has become accepted.
* There are cases where a word with ''[[un-]] -able'' is much more common than one with just ''-able'', such as ''[[unbreakable]]'', ''[[unsinkable]]'', and ''[[untouchable]]''.

